course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 3
title: Please, thank you, sorry, okay
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The busiest little phrase in Ukrainian is BUD laska: it means please, but also «you're welcome» and «here you are».
explain: Thank you is DYAkuyu, with the stress on the first syllable, and among friends just DYAka; the answer is BUD laska or neMA za shcho, «there's nothing to thank for».
explain: Sorry comes in sizes: vybaCHAI to a friend you bumped into, vybaCHte to a grown-up or a stranger, and proBAch when you really hurt someone after a quarrel.
explain: When Sashko's mum says «we'll go to the park after lunch», you can agree with DObre, LAdno or pevno — just watch out for LAdno said with a big sigh.
fact: BUD laska means please; it also means «you're welcome» and «here you are», so DYAkuyu is often answered with BUD laska.
fact: DYAkuyu means thank you; the stress falls on the first syllable, DYA-kuyu.
fact: duzhe DYAkuyu means thank you very much, for food, presents or a ride home.
fact: DYAka is a casual thanks between friends; to the friend's granny the full DYAkuyu is nicer.
fact: neMA za shcho means don't mention it, literally «there is nothing to thank for».
fact: vybaCHAI means sorry to one friend, for example after bumping into him; the «chai» in it has nothing to do with tea.
fact: vybaCHte means sorry or excuse me to adults and strangers, and it is also how you start a question to a stranger.
fact: proBAch means forgive me, a heartfelt sorry after a real quarrel; it is stronger than vybaCHAI.
fact: NIchoho, literally «nothing», is the usual answer to sorry: it's okay, never mind.
fact: LAdno means okay or fine, agreeing to a plan; said with a sigh it means «fine, whatever».
fact: DObre means good, and also okay or agreed; it is the polite okay to adults.
fact: pevno means surely or of course, a strong agreement.
exercise: What does BUD laska mean? | Please, and also you're welcome | Thank you very much, it was lovely | Sorry, my fault | Goodbye, take care
exercise: You hand Sashko his ball. Which phrase can mean «here you are»? | BUD laska | proBAch | NIchoho | LAdno
exercise: What does DYAkuyu mean? | Thank you | Excuse me, sorry | Please, go on | You're welcome
exercise: Where does the stress fall in DYAkuyu? | On the first syllable, DYA | On the middle syllable, KU | On the last syllable, YU | It has no stress at all
exercise: Which phrase thanks Granny warmly for a huge lunch? | duzhe DYAkuyu | DYAka | nE-a | vybaCHAI
exercise: Why is DYAkuyu nicer than DYAka with the friend's granny? | DYAka is the casual thanks for friends | DYAka means sorry, not thank you | DYAka is a rude word that grannies hate | DYAka means no
exercise: What does neMA za shcho mean? | Don't mention it | There is no food left | I have nothing at all | That is not for me, thanks
exercise: You bump into Sashko in the corridor. What do you say? | vybaCHAI | vybaCHte, pani | duzhe DYAkuyu | neMA za shcho
exercise: Why does vybaCHAI have nothing to do with tea? | The «chai» is just part of the word for sorry | It is said only at teatime, over a cup | It is a tea brand | It means «sorry about the tea», after spilling some
exercise: How do you politely start a question to a stranger in the street? | vybaCHte | zdorOv | proBAch | BUvai
exercise: When is proBAch the right sorry? | After a real quarrel, when you hurt a friend | When you bump into a stranger | When asking a shop assistant where the bread is | When you sneeze
exercise: Someone says vybaCHte to you. What is the usual answer? | NIchoho | BUD laska, nE-a | proBAch | DObroi NOchi
exercise: What does LAdno mean? | Okay, fine | Hello there | Lazy, sleepy | Thanks a lot
exercise: Why is DObre a good okay to adults? | It is the polite «okay, agreed» | It means «I'm bored» and is a bit cheeky | It means «fine, whatever» | It is only for grannies
exercise: What does pevno mean? | Surely, of course | Probably not | Perhaps later | Poor you
test: «BUD laska only means please, you can't say it after thanks» Which is right? | Wrong: BUD laska also answers DYAkuyu, as «you're welcome» | Right: after thanks you stay silent | Right: you must say proBAch | Wrong: after thanks you say BUvai
test: You trod on Sashko's brand-new Lego tower and he's really upset, still angry an hour later. Which sorry fits? | proBAch | vybaCHte | NIchoho | DYAka
test: «vybachai sounds like chai so it's what you say at teatime» Which is right? | No, vybaCHAI is a sorry to a friend | Yes, it means «tea, please» at the table | Yes, it is the thanks for tea | No, it means good morning
test: You ask Sashko to swap cards; he says «LAdno…» with a long sigh. What does he mean? | Fine, whatever — he's not happy | Brilliant, he loves the idea and can't wait | No way, never | He didn't hear you
test: Sashko's mum asks, «we'll eat at two, DObre?». Which reply fits? | DObre | nE-a, nikoli | proBAch, pani | zdorOv, BUvai
test: «saying neMA za shcho means there's nothing to eat» Which is right? | No, it means don't mention it | Yes, it is about an empty fridge at home | Yes, it means I'm hungry | No, it means I'm full and can't eat any more
test: What would happen if you said DYAka, not DYAkuyu, to the friend's grandad for a present? | Nothing terrible, but the full DYAkuyu sounds nicer to him | He would think you said sorry | It would be a swear word to him | He would take the present back
test: «just say sorry to a stranger with vybaCHAI, it's the same» What is right? | To an adult or stranger vybaCHte is the polite one | vybaCHAI is the only sorry there is | Strangers should get proBAch | You never say sorry to strangers, only to your friends
test: «ugh, vybaCHte, NIchoho, whatever, just give me the ball» What is the grumpy kid brushing off? | The polite sorry and its answer | A long goodbye at the front door | Thanks for the food | A question about his age
test: In a game the hero says «pevno!» when the king asks for help. What does he say? | Of course! | Probably not | Maybe later | I'm sorry, sire
test: «DYAkuyu — BUD laska — DYAkuyu — BUD laska…» Two kids keep going. Why does it work? | BUD laska is both please and the answer to thanks | They are saying hello and goodbye over and over again | They are counting | They are arguing
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression BUD laska? | It signifies please and is equally employed as a reply to thanks and when handing an object | It signifies thank you exclusively | It is an apology of considerable gravity | It is a valediction reserved for the evening
exam: Identify the apology appropriate to be addressed to an adult stranger, or to precede a question put to such a person. | vybaCHte | vybaCHAI | DYAka | LAdno
exam: Ascertain the circumstances in which proBAch is properly employed. | Following a genuine quarrel in which another person has been hurt | Upon a trivial collision with a stranger in a public thoroughfare | When soliciting the attention of a shopkeeper | When expressing gratitude for a meal
exam: Select the response consistent with the established usage of LAdno pronounced with a pronounced sigh. | It conveys reluctant consent, approximately «fine, whatever» | It conveys enthusiastic agreement and evident delight at the proposal | It constitutes a firm refusal | It is a greeting upon arrival
